WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.029 --> 00:00:09.360
Jack Babbitt:I was in Purdue University in
my first year there and I wanted to serve,

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:17.570
so I joined the Army Air Corps. Overseas as
a pilot in B17's, flew 29 combat missions

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:22.800
and a couple of missions dropping food to
the Dutch. Flew home on my birthday and the

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:29.689
war was over. That was my military career.
You've always heard there were no atheists

00:00:29.689 --> 00:00:34.280
in a foxhole, believe me there were no atheists
in an airplane either.

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:39.760
Jo Babbitt: Both my brothers, older brothers
were in the war. One was taken prisoner and

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:47.699
was in a German concentration camp. I remember
the impact that that had on our family and

00:00:47.699 --> 00:00:59.879
the importance of prayer at that time.
Jack Babbitt: One of the visits that started

00:00:59.879 --> 00:01:04.530
all of this was we were there on Sunday, that's
what led Jo to say, "Well, where are these

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:08.909
students worshiping? Where are they going
to church? There's a big need here."

00:01:08.909 --> 00:01:13.490
Jo Babbitt: It was interesting to me to think
that as you look at the campus you didn't

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:21.420
see the prominence of a place to worship where
they could go and just have a quiet meditation

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:28.100
time with the Lord. So I ask Mr. Cervini,
"Why not a chapel?" He said, "Well, interesting

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:33.719
you should mention that, that's something
for the future." To me, the future is now.

00:01:33.719 --> 00:01:40.829
Larry P. Arnn: Hillsdale has had a chapel
for most of its history. In the 1850's it

00:01:40.829 --> 00:01:46.659
built College Baptist Church down the street.
Well, we use the College Baptist Church today

00:01:46.659 --> 00:01:50.719
but the college is more than twice as big
as it was back then, we cannot all get into

00:01:50.719 --> 00:01:58.159
it anymore. College in America and medieval
times in Europe have always had academic religious

00:01:58.159 --> 00:02:04.369
ceremonies. We call the main ones here are
convocation. You need the whole campus community

00:02:04.369 --> 00:02:09.869
to get together and we don't have a place
to do that that's proper for it. Why do it

00:02:09.869 --> 00:02:15.360
now? Well, there are a lot of reasons, but
one of them is Mr. Babbitt and his wife Jo

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.870
are keen for us to build it. We're very grateful
for that and they've given us a significant

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:27.180
gift to get started on it. If we can get the
rest of the money, now is a better time than

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:32.540
tomorrow.
The two chief ways of knowing are reason and

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:38.790
faith and they are different ways of knowing.
There is some significant overlap between

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:45.030
them. There's never been a great university
that was not heavily concerned with the question

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:52.000
of God. There's never been serious Christian
practice that was not heavily concerned with

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:57.110
learning. To take one of them out is to deprive
it of an essential element. This chapel will

00:02:57.110 --> 00:03:05.040
be a daily reminder of that essential fact.
Always remembered here, never remembered too

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:10.890
much.
Duncan Stroik: Inspiration for the new Christ

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:17.549
chapel started with looking at Central Hall.
The yellow brick on the front porch, the two

00:03:17.549 --> 00:03:23.489
towers or pavilions to either side, the large
arched window in the middle is actually inspired

00:03:23.489 --> 00:03:29.239
by the round window at Central Hall which
is the spot where there was a chapel at one

00:03:29.239 --> 00:03:33.269
time, so it's where we started. What was the
inspiration for the inside? The interior of

00:03:33.269 --> 00:03:38.220
the chapel is inspired by that quintessential
English church St. Martin-in-the-Fields which

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:44.750
is the model for most early American churches.
Where you have a major nave, large columns

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:49.939
supporting a big vault and balconies, because
the college wanted to have a church that seats

00:03:49.939 --> 00:03:55.540
1,300 that would feel intimate for 600. The
balconies lead your eye towards the pulpit,

00:03:55.540 --> 00:04:00.989
towards the table, the altar, and there we
have an organ and a beautiful serliana window.

00:04:00.989 --> 00:04:05.299
So the interior is inspired by St. Martin-in-the-Fields,
and St. Martin-in-the-Fields is the model

00:04:05.299 --> 00:04:09.650
for such great churches as Christ Church in
Philadelphia where the Founding Fathers met

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:13.950
during the Continental Congress.
Megan Bowser: The liberal arts are concerned

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:19.079
with that which is true. So when you're studying
the liberal arts, and understanding the world

00:04:19.079 --> 00:04:23.310
around you and what it means to be human,
you come to the conclusion that to be human

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:27.199
is to be made in the image of God.
Leah Hickman: I see this chapel as being a

00:04:27.199 --> 00:04:33.330
visible reminder of God's presence on the
campus and his plan for what he wants the

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:37.389
students and faculty to do for his kingdom.
Drew French: I believe that building a chapel

00:04:37.389 --> 00:04:43.509
on this campus will be the perfect symbol
of what has been happening at Hillsdale for

00:04:43.509 --> 00:04:50.110
years. That is the serious pursuit of truth,
particularly in scripture and generally in

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:53.460
all of the liberal arts.
Larry P. Arnn: The college has four purposes

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:59.370
and they are: freedom, high learning that's
learning the ultimate goods in nature and

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:05.389
God, the development of high moral character,
and the propagation of the Christian faith.

00:05:05.389 --> 00:05:09.060
Those four things are central at Hillsdale
College, they have not been changed since

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:14.250
1844. So of course the chapel that has to
be right in the middle of everything.

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:19.690
Jo Babbitt: We just feel that the placement
of it is the absolute perfect place because

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:24.850
it just makes you aware of God's presence
on campus. You know, you go by and you cannot

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:30.789
help but think, "That's my faith, that's the
foundation that all of this learning is building

00:05:30.789 --> 00:05:32.880
to."
Larry P. Arnn: The first thing we're going

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:37.539
to do and it is we're going to have convocation
and baccalaureate. Convocation, there are

00:05:37.539 --> 00:05:43.340
four of them a year, including commencement.
We all gather, we wear our robes, the choir

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:49.919
sings, we say prayers. So there will also
be 30 prayer group meetings a day at Hillsdale

00:05:49.919 --> 00:05:56.430
College. There are 3 or 4 religious services
every day and those will be held in the chapel

00:05:56.430 --> 00:06:01.289
and in the side chapel, a home for those.
Then the chapel's going to be a great place

00:06:01.289 --> 00:06:07.520
for music. More than 30% of our students are
involved in music. We have an 85 piece orchestra,

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:13.090
we have 150 people in a chorus. They sing
and play a lot of sacred music, the chorus

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:20.360
mostly sacred music. We are going to put in
the chapel 2 world-class organs, and we want

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:28.270
to have choral evensong and the major orchestral
and choral performances will be in the chapel.

00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:33.970
I came here 16 years ago. The specific reason
that I decided that I would work here if they

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:38.960
would have me, and they had said that they
would, is that I read those beautiful original

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:44.020
articles of the college. Those articles were
written by New England preachers who'd come

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:48.699
to the frontier. Some of them became friends
of Abraham Lincoln and they did wonderful

00:06:48.699 --> 00:06:54.699
things in the cause of Lincoln. So as in the
founding, the practice of the Christian faith

00:06:54.699 --> 00:07:01.490
gave powerful support to the greatest movement
of freedom in all of human history. I came

00:07:01.490 --> 00:07:07.930
here with a love for that and a wish to make
that stronger and better. The chapel is actually

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:14.860
the last significant structure that requires
to be built to complete that work. The work

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:20.289
will never be complete, but the structures
will all be in place. Won't that be something?

00:07:20.289 --> 00:07:24.650
I pray for that.
Jack Babbitt: We didn't enter into this whole

00:07:24.650 --> 00:07:30.949
thing with Hillsdale very lightly. The Lord
has blessed us, more than we ever thought

00:07:30.949 --> 00:07:40.139
would b. With a blessing we also had the responsibility
to him to be good stewards because it's all

00:07:40.139 --> 00:07:46.069
his money, so where does this money go? We
prayed many times that this would be the proper

00:07:46.069 --> 00:07:52.830
place. We believe it is the proper place because
it will have students in there that are going

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:56.169
to change the world, and we need them, we
need them badly.

